d by sequences of 35 genes</article-title><source>Mol Biol Evol</source><year>2003</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>287</fpage><lpage>292</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12598696</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/molbev/msg040</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bapteste</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Brinkmann</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>DV</given-names></name><name><surname>Sensen</surname><given-names>CW</given-names></name><name><surname>Gordon</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Durufle</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Gaasterland</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Lopez</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Muller</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Philippe</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The analysis of 100 genes supports the grouping of three highly divergent amoebae: Dictyostelium, Entamoeba, and Mastigamoeba</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A</source><year>2002</year><volume>99</volume><fpage>1414</fpage><lpage>1419</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11830664</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1073/pnas.032662799</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rokas</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Williams</surname><given-names>BL</given-names></name><name><surname>King</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Carroll</surname><given-names>SB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Genome-scale approaches to resolving incongruence in molecular phylogenies</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>2003<